# ---- Ghost towns and mining ruins ----
N('garnet-ghost-town','Garnet ghost town','Garnet','mt','town','standing',46.82559,-113.33945,'site','public','walk','','1895–',
  'A gold camp in the Garnet Range. A stamp mill went up at First Chance Gulch in 1895 and a town grew round it, first called Mitchell and renamed Garnet in 1897. About a thousand people lived here in 1898, with saloons, stores, a barber shop and three hotels, but the boom was short and the town emptied when the hard-rock mines closed.',
  'The surviving buildings have been kept as they stand, in what the BLM calls one of Montana\'s best-preserved ghost towns, with a visitor center in summer. Wikipedia names the builders of the Dahl Saloon, Kelly\'s Saloon, the Hannifen House, the F.A. Davey Store and the Wells Hotel; which of these stand and which can be entered was not checked. Interior not checked.',
  'An 1890s gold town left high in the mountains.',
  'Federal land run by the Bureau of Land Management, Missoula Field Office (BLM page; the federal land map does not show it). From Montana Highway 200 between mile markers 22 and 23 take Garnet Range Road south: about 4 miles paved and about 9 more of gravel to the parking area; the BLM says the other routes in are steep and rough. Day use $10 a person, under 16 free (cash, check or card at the kiosk, or Recreation.gov). The visitor center is usually open daily 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. from May to September, weather permitting. From January to April the road is closed to wheeled vehicles and is reached only by snowmobile, skis or snowshoes. No camping within half a mile of the town. (BLM page, read November 2026.)',
  [W('Garnet, Montana'),['BLM: Garnet Ghost Town','https://www.blm.gov/visit/garnet-ghost-town']],
  th=[46.82693,-113.33637],thn='Garnet Ghost Town parking area (mapped lot)',brick='?'),
N('granite-ghost-town-union-hall','Granite ghost town: Miners Union Hall and Superintendent\'s House','Philipsburg','mt','town','ruins',46.317778,-113.248611,'area','public','walk','','1884–1893',
  'A silver town three miles above Philipsburg, laid out in 1884. At its height in 1889 the mines were producing $250,000 to $275,000 a month and about 3,000 people lived here. When the silver panic reached Granite on August 1, 1893, the National Register form says the town emptied within a day. Montana keeps the mine superintendent\'s house and the ruin of the Miners Union Hall as Granite Ghost Town State Park.',
  'The Miners Union Hall was finished in 1890: a three-storey building set into the hillside, with a ground floor and side walls of local granite and two upper storeys of red brick with granite window trim. In 1974 the roof had fallen in over the main hall and the interior was going to ruin, leaving the masonry shell. The Superintendent\'s House is a two-storey building of local granite with brick window heads and a brick chimney; it was standing by 1889. Their condition today was taken only from the FWP page, which calls the hall a ruin.',
  'The shell of a miners\' hall from a town that emptied in a day.',
  'Granite Ghost Town State Park, Montana FWP (FWP page; the federal land map does not show it). Open from Memorial Day to September 30; snow sometimes blocks the road into late May. The road up from Philipsburg is narrow, steep and winding and climbs 1,280 feet; FWP says a high-clearance vehicle is needed and the road is not suitable for RVs or buses.'+FWPF+' Who owns the rest of the townsite was not checked: stay within the park.',
  [W('Granite, Montana'),['Montana FWP: Granite Ghost Town State Park','https://fwp.mt.gov/stateparks/granite-ghost-town'],
   ['National Register nomination: Miners Union Hall',NR+'74001096_text'],['National Register nomination: Superintendent\'s House',NR+'74001097_text']],
  th=[46.31838,-113.25000],thn='Parking on Main Street, Granite (mapped lot)',brick='some'),
N('charter-oak-mine-mill','Charter Oak Mine and Mill','Elliston','mt','industry','standing',46.49067,-112.41913,'site','public','drive','','1930s–1990s',
  'A small gold, silver and lead mine in the Elliston district, on the Helena National Forest. A 50-ton flotation mill was built here on the site of an older mill; the National Register form dates it to about 1936–37, a history site to 1942. In 1943 the mine gave nearly all the ore of the district. Work went on into the Cold War years, the Forest Service acquired the site in 1995 and reclaimed it in the late 1990s.',
  'The timber flotation mill, 152 feet long, still holds its machinery: an ore load-out deck with its cable pulley, a jaw crusher, a Wilfley table and wooden flotation cells. Cabins, an assay office and a garage from the same years stand nearby; the National Register form lists 17 contributing buildings and 5 structures. The tailings pile was removed in 1996. Whether the buildings can be entered was not checked.',
  'A wartime mill with its machinery still inside.',
  'USDA Forest Service, Helena Ranger District. From Helena take U.S. 12 west about 22 miles to the Little Blackfoot River turnoff, then Forest Road 227 and 227 B-1. The National Register form (2000) says two gates on the last stretch were locked and needed a Forest Service key; a later history site describes them as open gates. Two tourism and history sites say the site is open to visitors on select summer weekends. The Forest Service page was not found: hours not checked, ask the Helena Ranger District before going.',
  [['National Register nomination (Forest Service, 2000)',NR+'01000038_text'],['Legends of America: Charter Oak Mine','https://www.legendsofamerica.com/charter-oak-mine-montana/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
# ---- Deer Lodge ----
N('old-montana-prison','Old Montana Prison','Deer Lodge','mt','inst','standing',46.39244,-112.73653,'site','public','walk','','1871–1979',
  'The territorial prison locked its first cell on July 2, 1871, and stayed the state\'s main prison until 1979, when the inmates moved to the new prison west of town. Under Warden Frank Conley (1890–1921) convicts built much of the compound, making about 1.2 million bricks by hand for the 1896 cell house and other buildings. A riot in 1959 held the prison for 36 hours until the National Guard went in.',
  'The walled compound with its corner turrets, the 1896 cell house, the slide-bar cells and the solitary cells of Maximum Security. Names, dates and prison numbers are scratched into the bricks in one corner of the yard. Guided and self-guided tours.',
  'A territorial prison its own inmates built, brick by brick.',
  'Run as a museum by the Powell County Museum and Arts Foundation (museum page), 1106 Main Street (visitor center at Main Street and Texas Avenue). Admission covers its five attractions: $20 adult, $12 ages 7 to 15, children 6 and under free. The museum page gave daily hours of 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. from October 1 to December 4, 2026, a winter closure from early December to mid-March, and closed Thanksgiving. Parking on Main Street or in the museum lot on Conley Avenue.',
  [W('Montana State Prison'),['Old Prison Museum: plan your visit','https://www.pcmaf.org/plan-your-visit'],['Old Prison Museum','https://www.pcmaf.org/old-prison-museum']],
  th=[46.39030,-112.73624],thn='Museum parking lot, Conley Avenue',brick='?'),
N('prison-brickyard-deer-lodge','Prison Brickyard','Deer Lodge','mt','industry','ruins',46.386667,-112.726944,'area','view','drive','','1915–1926',
  'Convicts made brick at the prison from about 1896; this yard was built in 1915 and worked until 1926, with one guard to about fifty prisoners. Its bricks went into state buildings across Montana from 1915 to 1919, among them the tuberculosis sanitarium at Galen. Falling demand after the First World War closed it, though stored bricks were sold into the early 1930s.',
  'In 1988 the yard held two ruined scove kilns of 1915 (the thick red brick walls of kilns about 70 by 28 feet), two beehive kilns, a smokestack with underground flues and a workshop built 1915–1922; the superintendent\'s brick house of 1918 and a small cottage stand across the road. What is left today was not checked.',
  'The yard where convicts fired the bricks for Montana\'s state buildings.',
  'In an open field about a quarter mile south of Deer Lodge on a county road. The 1988 National Register form gives it as state land; the owner now and whether visitors may walk in were not checked. Look from the road.',
  [['National Register nomination (1988)',NR+'88000430_text']],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('grant-kohrs-ranch','Grant-Kohrs Ranch','Deer Lodge','mt','town','standing',46.408667,-112.737975,'site','public','walk','','1862–',
  'The fur trader Johnny Grant built a large two-storey house here in 1862. Conrad Kohrs bought it with its furniture in 1866 and, with his half-brother John Bielenberg, built the home ranch of a cattle business that in the 1890s ran over 27,000 acres and grazed on a million acres of public range. Congress made it a national historic site in 1972.',
  'The Grant house, log-framed and sided, with the two-storey brick addition Kohrs finished by 1890 and the conservatory added by 1907; the furnishings of the public rooms downstairs are mostly original. Around it stand the bunkhouse, buggy shed, ice house, granary, barns, cow sheds and corrals built between 1862 and 1919. Ranch house tours, and nearly ten miles of ranch roads and trails.',
  'A working cattle ranch kept as it was.',
  'National Park Service. Open daily except Thanksgiving, December 25 and January 1; no entrance fee. Visitor contact station and parking at the north end of Deer Lodge.',
  [['NPS: Grant-Kohrs Ranch','https://www.nps.gov/grko/index.htm'],['NPS: hours','https://www.nps.gov/grko/planyourvisit/hours.htm'],
   ['National Register nomination',NR+'72000738_text']],
  th=[46.40562,-112.73607],thn='Park parking area, Grant Circle',brick='?'),
N('rialto-theater-deer-lodge','Rialto Theater','Deer Lodge','mt','civic','standing',46.400556,-112.735556,'site','view','drive','','1921–',
  'A 720-seat Beaux Arts theater of 1921, with painted murals and plasterwork inside and white terra cotta and red roof tiles outside. A community nonprofit bought it in 1995. A fire on November 4, 2006 gutted the stage, auditorium and balcony; the town rebuilt it as a historical reproduction and reopened it in May 2012.',
  'The 1921 terra cotta front. The interior is a 2012 reproduction after the fire. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Run by a community nonprofit as a theater. Open when there is a show; schedule not checked. Seen from Main Street.',
  [W('Rialto Theater (Deer Lodge, Montana)')],brick='?'),
# ---- Anaconda ----
N('anaconda-smelter-stack','Anaconda Smelter Stack','Anaconda','mt','industry','standing',46.11002,-112.91375,'exact','view','drive','','1918–1980',
  'The chimney of the Anaconda Copper Mining Company\'s Washoe Smelter, the tallest surviving masonry structure in the world: FWP gives 585 feet. Wikipedia says it was built in 1918, FWP that it was completed in 1919; Wikipedia says the smelter closed in 1981, FWP in 1980.',
  'A brick chimney on a concrete base. A terra cotta skin covered it when new; most has eroded away except on the upper part, showing the bricks. It may be seen and photographed only from a distance; interpretive signs are at the viewing site near Goodman Park.',
  'Taller than the Washington Monument, and you may only look.',
  'Anaconda Smoke Stack State Park, Montana FWP. The stack itself is closed; the viewing site (100 Smelter Road) is open daily all year in daylight hours. (FWP page, read November 2026.)',
  [W('Anaconda Smelter Stack'),['Montana FWP: Anaconda Smoke Stack State Park','https://fwp.mt.gov/stateparks/anaconda-smoke-stack']],
  conf='mixed',th=[46.12319,-112.93143],thn='Viewing site near Goodman Park, Smelter Road',brick='?'),
N('glenns-dam-anaconda','Glenn\'s Dam (Hefner\'s)','Anaconda','mt','water','ruins',46.14173,-112.98293,'area','public','walk','','1883–1902',
  'In 1883, while the first Anaconda smelter was going up, workers built a headrace, a reservoir and two earth dams on Warm Springs Creek, and a pipe carried the water two miles down to the Upper Works. The dam, finished in 1884 and named for its first watchman William Glenn, had a 540-foot earth embankment. The company gave it up in 1902 when the new Washoe Smelter got a larger water system; after 1959 the dam was breached and the reservoir partly drained.',
  'The breached earth dam, the reservoir pond seen from a walking trail, and the restored Hefner\'s pump house with its National Register plaque beside a bridge over Warm Springs Creek.',
  '',
  'The Montana historic preservation office gives it as a public trail on North Cable Road, a quarter mile northwest of Anaconda; the county took the photographs. Who manages the trail and its hours were not checked.',
  [['Historic Montana: Glenn\'s Dam Historic District','https://historicmt.org/items/show/3044']],
  th=[46.13994,-112.98077],thn='Parking lot on Cable Road (mapped lot)',brick='?'),
N('washoe-theater-anaconda','Washoe Theater','Anaconda','mt','civic','standing',46.1275,-112.952222,'site','view','drive','','1930–',
  'A movie palace designed in 1930 by the Seattle theater architect B. Marcus Priteca and almost finished by 1931, but the Depression held its opening to September 24, 1936. Wikipedia calls it the last theater built in the country in the Nuevo Deco style. It was listed on the National Register in 1982.',
  'Etched glass doors, carved relief on every joint and trim, ornamental iron, and much copper, with silver and gold leaf. Every flat surface, the dome included, is painted in murals by Colville Smythe, and the silk curtain is original. Seen inside when films are shown.',
  'A copper-town movie palace with its 1936 interior intact.',
  'A working movie theater at 305 Main Street (Visit Montana listing). Show times and whether tours are given were not checked.',
  [W('Washoe Theater'),['Visit Montana: Washoe Theater','https://www.visitmt.com/listings/general/movie-theatre/washoe-theater']],brick='?'),
N('club-moderne-anaconda','Club Moderne','Anaconda','mt','civic','standing',46.127778,-112.943889,'site','view','drive','','1937–',
  'A Streamline Moderne bar designed by Fred F. Willson and built in 1937, with a front of Carrara glass. It was voted America\'s favorite historic bar in a National Trust contest in 2016, and badly damaged by fire that October 3. It reopened on May 13, 2017.',
  'After the fire much of the interior was replaced and the outside refurbished; the bar itself, a few original furnishings and some of the original front were kept.',
  '',
  'A working bar, privately owned. Hours not checked. Seen from the street.',
  [W('Club Moderne')],brick='?'),
N('anaconda-city-hall','Anaconda City Hall','Anaconda','mt','civic','standing',46.129444,-112.9475,'site','public','walk','','1895–',
  'Built in 1895 to designs by Collins W. Reber of Charles Lane & Company, of Anaconda pressed brick and granite trimmed with Anaconda copper, with a clock tower, a treasurer\'s office and a fire station. It was threatened with demolition in 1978 and saved by a local group that went to court.',
  'The 1895 front with its clock tower. Inside are the Copper Village Museum and Arts Center and the Marcus Daly Historical Society museum and archives; what original interior survives was not checked.',
  '',
  '401 East Commercial Avenue, now the City Hall Cultural Center. A tourism listing (Southwest Montana) calls the Copper Village Museum free and gives Tuesday to Friday, 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; the museum\'s own page was not read.',
  [W('Anaconda City Hall'),['Southwest Montana: Copper Village Museum and Arts Center','https://southwestmt.com/listings/copper-village-museum-and-arts-center']],brick='?'),
